# Submission

What a model hands back, and how it becomes a document the kernel checks.

A model answers with prose and exactly one fenced block of LOGOS kernel
vernacular, opened by a line ```` ```logos-kernel ````. The block is the
SUBMISSION. The factory never runs it as written: it assembles the proof
document itself —

1. the accepted STATEMENT document, byte for byte (its hash is what a result is
   bound to), which defines the goal as a `Prop`;
2. the submission;
3. two closing checks the factory appends: `Check <proof>.`, whose output must be
   exactly `<proof> : <goal>`, and `Print Axioms <proof>.`, whose output the
   acceptance policy reads.

The kernel decides whether the document checks. What the kernel does NOT decide
is whether the submission changed the meaning of the words the statement uses:
the vernacular lets a `Definition` shadow any existing name, prelude names
included (`LOGOS_ISSUES.md`, issue 2). So the submission is also read here, before
the kernel sees it, and refused when it uses a command that changes how text
reads, declares a name twice, or does not contain the one theorem it must. Every
name it declares is then probed against the statement and the prelude by the
verifier, and a name that already exists refuses the submission.

To read a submission the way the kernel does, this module splits a document into
blocks exactly as the kernel's document door does — a comment is a block, a
command ends at a `.` followed by whitespace — so block numbers here are the block
numbers the kernel reports, and a failed block can be shown back to the model.

## Note
The bytes the splitter cares about, by name.

## To isSpaceByte (b: Int) -> Bool:
    Return b is equal to 32 or b is equal to 9 or b is equal to 10 or b is equal to 13.

## Note
The byte at a 1-based position, or `0` past either end.

## To byteAt (bs: Seq of Int, i: Int) -> Int:
    If i is less than 1 or i is greater than length of bs:
        Return 0.
    Return item i of bs.

## Note
The last byte of a `/- … -/` comment that opens at `start`, with nesting as Lean and
the kernel count it, or `-1` when it never closes.

## To commentEnd (bs: Seq of Int, start: Int) -> Int:
    Let n be length of bs.
    Let mutable depth be 0.
    Let mutable i be start.
    While i is less than n:
        Let b be item i of bs.
        Let c be item (i + 1) of bs.
        If b is equal to 47 and c is equal to 45:
            Set depth to depth + 1.
            Set i to i + 2.
        Otherwise:
            If b is equal to 45 and c is equal to 47:
                Set depth to depth - 1.
                If depth is equal to 0:
                    Return i + 1.
                Set i to i + 2.
            Otherwise:
                Set i to i + 1.
    Return 0 - 1.

## Note
The last byte of a command that starts at `start`: the first `.` outside a string
and outside a «quoted name» that is followed by whitespace or the end. This is the
kernel's `sentence_end`, byte for byte.

## To sentenceEnd (bs: Seq of Int, start: Int) -> Int:
    Let n be length of bs.
    Let mutable inString be false.
    Let mutable inName be false.
    Let mutable escaped be false.
    Let mutable i be start.
    While i is at most n:
        Let b be item i of bs.
        If escaped:
            Set escaped to false.
        Otherwise:
            If inName:
                If b is equal to 194 and byteAt(bs, i + 1) is equal to 187:
                    Set inName to false.
                    Set i to i + 1.
            Otherwise:
                If b is equal to 194 and byteAt(bs, i + 1) is equal to 171 and inString is equal to false:
                    Set inName to true.
                    Set i to i + 1.
                Otherwise:
                    If b is equal to 92 and inString:
                        Set escaped to true.
                    Otherwise:
                        If b is equal to 34:
                            Set inString to not inString.
                        Otherwise:
                            If b is equal to 46 and inString is equal to false:
                                If i is equal to n or isSpaceByte(byteAt(bs, i + 1)):
                                    Return i.
        Set i to i + 1.
    Return n.

## Note
A document split into the kernel's blocks, or the reason it cannot be: an
unterminated comment, which the kernel refuses too.

## A Blocks has:
    an items, which is Seq of Text.
    a problem, which is Text.

## Note
The blocks of a document, in order, each trimmed: comments (`-- …` to the end of
its line, `/- … -/` nested) and commands.

## To kernelBlocks (doc: Text) -> Blocks:
    Let bs be text_bytes(doc).
    Let n be length of bs.
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Text.
    Let mutable i be 1.
    While i is at most n:
        While i is at most n and isSpaceByte(item i of bs):
            Set i to i + 1.
        If i is at most n:
            Let b be item i of bs.
            Let c be byteAt(bs, i + 1).
            Let mutable last be 0.
            If b is equal to 45 and c is equal to 45:
                Set last to i.
                While last is less than n and item last of bs is not equal to 10:
                    Set last to last + 1.
            Otherwise:
                If b is equal to 47 and c is equal to 45:
                    Set last to commentEnd(bs, i).
                    If last is less than 0:
                        Return a new Blocks with items out and problem "an unterminated `/-` comment opens block {length of out + 1}".
                Otherwise:
                    Set last to sentenceEnd(bs, i).
            Push trim(text_from_bytes(items i through last of bs)) to out.
            Set i to last + 1.
    Return a new Blocks with items out and problem "".

## Note
Whether a block is a comment.

## To isCommentBlock (block: Text) -> Bool:
    Return startsWith(block, "--") or startsWith(block, "/-").

## Note
The words of a block, split on whitespace.

## To blockWords (block: Text) -> Seq of Text:
    Return words(block).

## Note
The command a block is: its first word, after the visibility words the kernel
reads past (`Local`, `Private`, `Protected`). A comment is `comment`, and the
literate forms the kernel also reads (`## To …`, `## Theorem: …`, `Let x be …`,
`A Nat is either …`) are `literate`.

## To commandOf (block: Text) -> Text:
    If isCommentBlock(block):
        Return "comment".
    If startsWith(block, "##"):
        Return "literate".
    Let ws be blockWords(block).
    If length of ws is equal to 0:
        Return "empty".
    Let mutable i be 1.
    While i is less than length of ws and ["Local", "Private", "Protected"] contains item i of ws:
        Set i to i + 1.
    Let w be item i of ws.
    If w is equal to "Let" or ((w is equal to "A" or w is equal to "An") and block contains " is either"):
        Return "literate".
    Return w.

## Note
The commands a submission may use: declarations that add names, and the two
queries. Everything else the vernacular offers — notation, macros, fixity,
namespaces, sections, `Open`, `Export`, `Structure`, `Quotient`, `Opaque` and the
literate forms — changes how later text reads or adds names this module does not
track, so a submission that uses one is refused rather than reasoned about.

## To submissionCommands () -> Seq of Text:
    Return ["comment", "Definition", "Theorem", "Axiom", "Inductive", "Check", "Eval", "Print"].

## Note
The commands a statement document may use: definitions and inductive types only.
A statement asserts nothing, so it has no `Axiom` and no `Theorem`.

## To statementCommands () -> Seq of Text:
    Return ["comment", "Definition", "Inductive", "Check", "Eval"].

## Note
Whether a byte can be part of a declaration name: letters, digits, `_`, `'`, `.`
for hierarchy, and any byte of a multi-byte UTF-8 character (`α`, `«…»`).

## To isNameByte (b: Int) -> Bool:
    If b is at least 128:
        Return true.
    If b is at least 48 and b is at most 57:
        Return true.
    If b is at least 65 and b is at most 90:
        Return true.
    If b is at least 97 and b is at most 122:
        Return true.
    Return b is equal to 95 or b is equal to 39 or b is equal to 46.

## Note
The name that starts at the first non-space byte at or after `start`, and where
it ends. A universe list `.{u}` ends a name, as does anything that is not a name
byte. Answers `(name, position after it)`.

## To nameAt (bs: Seq of Int, start: Int) -> (Text, Int):
    Let n be length of bs.
    Let mutable i be start.
    While i is at most n and isSpaceByte(item i of bs):
        Set i to i + 1.
    Let first be i.
    While i is at most n and isNameByte(item i of bs) and (item i of bs is not equal to 46 or byteAt(bs, i + 1) is not equal to 123):
        Set i to i + 1.
    If i is equal to first:
        Return ("", i).
    Let mutable last be i - 1.
    While last is at least first and item last of bs is equal to 46:
        Set last to last - 1.
    Return (text_from_bytes(items first through last of bs), i).

## Note
The byte position right after the command word (and the visibility words before
it) of a block.

## To afterCommandWord (bs: Seq of Int, word: Text) -> Int:
    Let wb be text_bytes(word).
    Let n be length of bs.
    Let m be length of wb.
    Let mutable i be 1.
    While i + m - 1 is at most n:
        If bytesMatchAt(bs, wb, i):
            Return i + m.
        Set i to i + 1.
    Return n + 1.

## Note
The constructors of an inductive block, and the names of the further types of a
mutual block: after each top-level `:=`, each alternative separated by `|` starts
with a constructor name, and a top-level `with` starts the next type of the block.

## To inductiveNames (bs: Seq of Int, from: Int) -> Seq of Text:
    Let n be length of bs.
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Text.
    Let mutable depth be 0.
    Let mutable inBody be false.
    Let mutable atAlternative be false.
    Let mutable i be from.
    While i is at most n:
        Let b be item i of bs.
        If b is equal to 40 or b is equal to 91 or b is equal to 123:
            Set depth to depth + 1.
        If b is equal to 41 or b is equal to 93 or b is equal to 125:
            Set depth to depth - 1.
        If depth is equal to 0:
            If b is equal to 58 and byteAt(bs, i + 1) is equal to 61:
                Set inBody to true.
                Set atAlternative to true.
                Set i to i + 1.
            Otherwise:
                If b is equal to 124 and inBody:
                    Set atAlternative to true.
                Otherwise:
                    If inBody and b is equal to 119 and bytesMatchAt(bs, text_bytes("with "), i) and isSpaceByte(byteAt(bs, i - 1)):
                        Let (other, next) be nameAt(bs, i + 4).
                        If other is not equal to "":
                            Push other to out.
                        Set inBody to false.
                        Set atAlternative to false.
                        Set i to next - 1.
                    Otherwise:
                        If atAlternative and isSpaceByte(b) is equal to false:
                            Let (ctor, after) be nameAt(bs, i).
                            If ctor is not equal to "":
                                Push ctor to out.
                            Set atAlternative to false.
                            Set i to after - 1.
        Set i to i + 1.
    Return out.

## Note
The names a block declares: the declared name of a `Definition`, `Theorem`,
`Axiom` or `Opaque`, and for an `Inductive` its name, its constructors and the
other types of a mutual block. A query or a comment declares nothing.

## To declaredNames (block: Text) -> Seq of Text:
    Let command be commandOf(block).
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Text.
    If ["Definition", "Theorem", "Axiom", "Opaque", "Inductive"] contains command is equal to false:
        Return out.
    Let bs be text_bytes(block).
    Let (name, after) be nameAt(bs, afterCommandWord(bs, command)).
    If name is not equal to "":
        Push name to out.
    If command is equal to "Inductive":
        Repeat for more in inductiveNames(bs, after):
            Push more to out.
    Return out.

## Note
The text after the first top-level `:=` of a block, trimmed, without the closing
period: a definition's body, a theorem's proof.

## To bodyOf (block: Text) -> Text:
    Let parts be splitN(block, ":=", 2).
    If length of parts is less than 2:
        Return "".
    Return removeSuffix(trim(item 2 of parts), ".").

## Note
The text between the declared name and the first `:=` of a block, trimmed: a
theorem's statement, with any `:` in front of it removed.

## To headTypeOf (block: Text) -> Text:
    Let head be item 1 of splitN(block, ":=", 2).
    Let parts be splitN(head, ":", 2).
    If length of parts is less than 2:
        Return "".
    Return trim(item 2 of parts).

## Note
The steps of a theorem's tactic script (`by intro n; rfl.`), each its first word:
the SHAPE of the proof, with every name and term taken out. A proof by a term is
the one step `term`.

## To tacticSkeleton (block: Text) -> Seq of Text:
    Let body be bodyOf(block).
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Text.
    If startsWith(body, "by ") is equal to false:
        Push "term" to out.
        Return out.
    Repeat for step in split(removePrefix(body, "by "), ";"):
        Let ws be words(step).
        If length of ws is greater than 0:
            Push item 1 of ws to out.
    Return out.

## Note
The skeleton of a whole submission: every theorem's steps in order, theorems
separated by ` | `. Two submissions that differ only in names, terms or spacing
have one skeleton, which is what an approach fingerprint needs.

## To submissionSkeleton (submission: Text) -> Text:
    Let mutable parts be a new Seq of Text.
    Repeat for block in (kernelBlocks(submission))'s items:
        If commandOf(block) is equal to "Theorem":
            Push join(tacticSkeleton(block), ";") to parts.
    Return join(parts, " | ").

## Note
Every name a submission declares, in order, repeats included.

## To submissionNames (submission: Text) -> Seq of Text:
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Text.
    Repeat for block in (kernelBlocks(submission))'s items:
        Repeat for name in declaredNames(block):
            Push name to out.
    Return out.

## Note
The theorem a submission must contain to prove the goal `goalName`.

## To proofNameFor (goalName: Text) -> Text:
    Return "proof_of_" + goalName.

## Note
Everything wrong with a submission that can be seen without the kernel, or the
empty list. `proofName` is the theorem it must contain, and `goalName` the type
that theorem must have.

## To submissionProblems (submission: Text, proofName: Text, goalName: Text) -> Seq of Text:
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Text.
    Let blocks be kernelBlocks(submission).
    If blocks's problem is not equal to "":
        Push blocks's problem to out.
        Return out.
    If length of blocks's items is equal to 0:
        Push "the submission is empty" to out.
        Return out.
    Let mutable proofs be 0.
    Let mutable index be 0.
    Repeat for block in blocks's items:
        Set index to index + 1.
        Let command be commandOf(block).
        If submissionCommands() contains command is equal to false:
            Push "block {index} uses `{command}`, which a submission may not use" to out.
        If command is equal to "Theorem":
            Let names be declaredNames(block).
            If length of names is greater than 0 and item 1 of names is equal to proofName:
                Set proofs to proofs + 1.
                If headTypeOf(block) is not equal to goalName:
                    Push "the theorem {proofName} must have type exactly `{goalName}`, not `{headTypeOf(block)}`" to out.
    If proofs is equal to 0:
        Push "the submission has no `Theorem {proofName} : {goalName} := …`" to out.
    Let names be submissionNames(submission).
    Let mutable seen be a new Seq of Text.
    Repeat for name in names:
        If seen contains name:
            Push "the name {name} is declared twice" to out.
        Otherwise:
            Push name to seen.
        If name is equal to goalName:
            Push "the submission redeclares the goal {goalName}" to out.
    Return out.

## Note
Everything wrong with a statement document that can be seen without the kernel:
only definitions and inductive types, and the goal defined as a `Prop`.

## To statementProblems (document: Text, goalName: Text) -> Seq of Text:
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Text.
    Let blocks be kernelBlocks(document).
    If blocks's problem is not equal to "":
        Push blocks's problem to out.
        Return out.
    Let mutable goals be 0.
    Let mutable index be 0.
    Repeat for block in blocks's items:
        Set index to index + 1.
        Let command be commandOf(block).
        If statementCommands() contains command is equal to false:
            Push "block {index} uses `{command}`, which a statement may not use" to out.
        Let names be declaredNames(block).
        If command is equal to "Definition" and length of names is greater than 0 and item 1 of names is equal to goalName:
            Set goals to goals + 1.
            If headTypeOf(block) is not equal to "Prop":
                Push "the goal {goalName} must be defined as a `Prop`" to out.
    If goals is not equal to 1:
        Push "the statement must define `{goalName}` exactly once, as `Definition {goalName} : Prop := …`" to out.
    Return out.

## Note
The proof document the kernel checks: the statement, the submission, and the
factory's two closing checks.

## To assembleProofDocument (statement: Text, submission: Text, proofName: Text) -> Text:
    Return trimEnd(statement) + "\n\n/-! ## Submission -/\n\n" + trim(submission) + "\n\n/-! ## Closing checks, appended by the factory -/\n\nCheck " + proofName + ".\n\nPrint Axioms " + proofName + ".\n".

## Note
The document that asks the kernel whether `name` is already bound after
`preamble` (the statement document, or nothing for the bare prelude): it checks
exactly when the name exists.

## To nameProbeDocument (preamble: Text, name: Text) -> Text:
    If trim(preamble) is equal to "":
        Return "Check " + name + ".\n".
    Return trimEnd(preamble) + "\n\nCheck " + name + ".\n".

## Note
The text of block `k` of a document, as the kernel numbers blocks, or `""`.

## To blockText (doc: Text, k: Int) -> Text:
    Let blocks be (kernelBlocks(doc))'s items.
    If k is less than 1 or k is greater than length of blocks:
        Return "".
    Return item k of blocks.

## Note
What an extraction found: the submission, the summary line, the suggestions, or
why there is no submission.

## An Extraction has:
    a block, which is Text.
    a summary, which is Text.
    a suggestions, which is Seq of Text.
    a problem, which is Text.

## Note
Whether a line opens a fence with the given info string.

## To opensFence (line: Text, info: Text) -> Bool:
    Let t be trim(line).
    If startsWith(t, "```") is equal to false:
        Return false.
    Return trim(trimChars(t, "`")) is equal to info.

## Note
Whether a line closes a fence: nothing but three or more backticks.

## To closesFence (line: Text) -> Bool:
    Let t be trim(line).
    Return startsWith(t, "```") and trimChars(t, "`") is equal to "".

## Note
The submission inside a model's answer.

The output contract: exactly one fenced block whose info string is
`logos-kernel`. Fences with any other info string (an example in prose) are
ignored. Outside the block, a line `Summary: …` is the attempt's one-line
account of itself, and each line `Next: …` is a suggestion for the attempts after
it; both go onto the trail. Zero blocks, two blocks or an unclosed block is a
refusal, never a guess.

## To extractSubmission (answer: Text) -> Extraction:
    Let mutable found be 0.
    Let mutable inside be false.
    Let mutable inOther be false.
    Let mutable body be a new Seq of Text.
    Let mutable summary be "".
    Let mutable suggestions be a new Seq of Text.
    Repeat for line in lines(answer):
        If inside:
            If closesFence(line):
                Set inside to false.
            Otherwise:
                If found is equal to 1:
                    Push line to body.
        Otherwise:
            If inOther:
                If closesFence(line):
                    Set inOther to false.
            Otherwise:
                If opensFence(line, "logos-kernel"):
                    Set found to found + 1.
                    Set inside to true.
                Otherwise:
                    If startsWith(trim(line), "```"):
                        Set inOther to true.
                    Otherwise:
                        Let t be trim(line).
                        If startsWith(t, "Summary:"):
                            Set summary to trim(removePrefix(t, "Summary:")).
                        If startsWith(t, "Next:"):
                            Push trim(removePrefix(t, "Next:")) to suggestions.
    Let mutable problem be "".
    If inside:
        Set problem to "the ```logos-kernel block is never closed".
    If found is equal to 0:
        Set problem to "the answer has no ```logos-kernel block".
    If found is greater than 1:
        Set problem to "the answer has {found} ```logos-kernel blocks; exactly one is allowed".
    Let mutable block be "".
    If problem is equal to "":
        Set block to trim(join(body, "\n")).
    Return a new Extraction with block block and summary summary and suggestions suggestions and problem problem.

## Note
Lines joined into one text, one element a line (`""` for a blank line).

A multi-line text literal inside an indented block is misread once the program
also reaches `crypto.lg` (`LOGOS_ISSUES.md`, issue 7), and the factory does, so a
text of several lines is written as its lines.

## To joinLines (xs: Seq of Text) -> Text:
    Return join(xs, "\n").

## Note
The statement of the kernel fixtures in `tests/fixtures/kernel/`.

## To fixtureStatement () -> Text:
    Return joinLines(["/-! # Statement: zero is a left identity of addition on Nat", "", "Route C. A proof must establish `goal`. -/", "", "Definition goal : Prop := forall (n : Nat), Eq Nat (Nat_add Zero n) n."]).

## Note
The submission of `tests/fixtures/kernel/verified.lg`.

## To fixtureProof () -> Text:
    Return joinLines(["Theorem succ_step : forall (k : Nat), Eq Nat (Nat_add Zero (Succ k)) (Succ (Nat_add Zero k)) := by intro k; rfl.", "", "Theorem proof_of_goal : goal := by intro n; induction n as [ | k ih]; rfl; rw [succ_step k]; rw [ih]; rfl."]).

## Test: a document splits into the kernel's blocks, comments included

Let blocks be kernelBlocks(assembleProofDocument(fixtureStatement(), fixtureProof(), "proof_of_goal")).
Expect that blocks's problem is equal to "".
Expect that length of blocks's items is equal to 8.
Expect that item 2 of blocks's items is equal to "Definition goal : Prop := forall (n : Nat), Eq Nat (Nat_add Zero n) n.".
Expect that item 7 of blocks's items is equal to "Check proof_of_goal.".
Expect that item 8 of blocks's items is equal to "Print Axioms proof_of_goal.".

## Test: the assembled document is the recorded fixture, byte for byte

Let doc be assembleProofDocument(fixtureStatement(), fixtureProof(), "proof_of_goal").
Let expected be """/-! # Statement: zero is a left identity of addition on Nat

Route C. A proof must establish `goal`. -/

Definition goal : Prop := forall (n : Nat), Eq Nat (Nat_add Zero n) n.

/-! ## Submission -/

Theorem succ_step : forall (k : Nat), Eq Nat (Nat_add Zero (Succ k)) (Succ (Nat_add Zero k)) := by intro k; rfl.

Theorem proof_of_goal : goal := by intro n; induction n as [ | k ih]; rfl; rw [succ_step k]; rw [ih]; rfl.

/-! ## Closing checks, appended by the factory -/

Check proof_of_goal.

Print Axioms proof_of_goal.""" + "\n".
Expect that doc is equal to expected.

## Test: a period inside a name or a string does not end a command

Let blocks be (kernelBlocks("Definition Lean.Eq.rec.{{u}} : Prop := True.\nCheck «a. b».\nEval \"x. y\"."))'s items.
Expect that length of blocks is equal to 3.
Expect that item 2 of blocks is equal to "Check «a. b».".
Expect that item 3 of blocks is equal to "Eval \"x. y\".".

## Test: a nested comment is one block and an unclosed one is refused

Let ok be kernelBlocks("/- outer /- inner -/ still outer -/\nCheck Zero.").
Expect that length of ok's items is equal to 2.
Expect that item 1 of ok's items is equal to "/- outer /- inner -/ still outer -/".
Let bad be kernelBlocks("Check Zero.\n/- never closed").
Expect that bad's problem is equal to "an unterminated `/-` comment opens block 2".

## Test: a line comment runs to the end of its line

Let blocks be (kernelBlocks("-- a note. with a period\nCheck Zero."))'s items.
Expect that blocks is equal to ["-- a note. with a period", "Check Zero."].

## Test: the command of a block is read past visibility words

For each of these cases:

| block                                        | command      |
|----------------------------------------------|--------------|
| "Definition one : Nat := Succ Zero."         | "Definition" |
| "Private Definition x : Nat := Zero."        | "Definition" |
| "Local Notation \"a ++ b\" := (f a b)."      | "Notation"   |
| "-- note"                                    | "comment"    |
| "## Theorem: T"                              | "literate"   |
| "Let x be 5."                                | "literate"   |
| "A Colour is either Red or Blue."            | "literate"   |
| "Print Axioms t."                            | "Print"      |

Expect that commandOf(block) is equal to command.

## Test: declarations name what they declare

Expect that declaredNames("Theorem proof_of_goal : goal := by rfl.") is equal to ["proof_of_goal"].
Expect that declaredNames("Definition Lean.Eq.rec.{{u, v}} : Prop := True.") is equal to ["Lean.Eq.rec"].
Expect that declaredNames("Axiom h1f : Nat -> Nat.") is equal to ["h1f"].
Expect that declaredNames("Definition two:Nat := Succ (Succ Zero).") is equal to ["two"].
Expect that declaredNames("Check two.") is equal to [].

## Test: an inductive declares its constructors and its mutual partners

Expect that declaredNames("Inductive MyBool := Yes : MyBool | No : MyBool.") is equal to ["MyBool", "Yes", "No"].
Expect that declaredNames("Inductive Leq (n : Nat) : Nat -> Prop := leq_n : Leq n n | leq_S : (m : Nat) -> Leq n m -> Leq n (Succ m).") is equal to ["Leq", "leq_n", "leq_S"].
Expect that declaredNames("Inductive Even : Nat -> Prop := ev0 : Even Zero | evS : (n : Nat) -> Odd n -> Even (Succ n) with Odd : Nat -> Prop := odS : (n : Nat) -> Even n -> Odd (Succ n).") is equal to ["Even", "ev0", "evS", "Odd", "odS"].

## Test: a skeleton keeps the shape of a proof and drops its names

Expect that tacticSkeleton("Theorem p : goal := by intro n; induction n as [ | k ih]; rfl; rw [succ_step k]; rw [ih]; rfl.") is equal to ["intro", "induction", "rfl", "rw", "rw", "rfl"].
Expect that tacticSkeleton("Theorem p : goal := fun (n : Nat) => refl Nat n.") is equal to ["term"].
Expect that submissionSkeleton(fixtureProof()) is equal to "intro;rfl | intro;induction;rfl;rw;rw;rfl".
Expect that submissionSkeleton("Theorem a : goal := by intro m; rfl.") is equal to submissionSkeleton("Theorem b  :  goal :=  by intro z ;  rfl.").

## Test: the fixture proof is a well-formed submission

Expect that proofNameFor("goal") is equal to "proof_of_goal".
Expect that submissionProblems(fixtureProof(), "proof_of_goal", "goal") is equal to [].
Expect that submissionNames(fixtureProof()) is equal to ["succ_step", "proof_of_goal"].

## Test: a submission that changes how text reads is refused before the kernel sees it

Let s be "Notation \"a + b\" := (Nat_add b a).\n\nTheorem proof_of_goal : goal := by intro n; rfl.".
Expect that submissionProblems(s, "proof_of_goal", "goal") is equal to ["block 1 uses `Notation`, which a submission may not use"].

## Test: a submission must prove exactly the goal

Expect that submissionProblems("Theorem other : goal := by intro n; rfl.", "proof_of_goal", "goal") is equal to ["the submission has no `Theorem proof_of_goal : goal := …`"].
Expect that submissionProblems("Theorem proof_of_goal : True := by exact I.", "proof_of_goal", "goal") is equal to ["the theorem proof_of_goal must have type exactly `goal`, not `True`"].

## Test: a submission may not redeclare the goal or declare a name twice

Let s be "Definition goal : Prop := True.\n\nTheorem proof_of_goal : goal := by exact I.\n\nTheorem proof_of_goal : goal := by exact I.".
Expect that submissionProblems(s, "proof_of_goal", "goal") is equal to ["the submission redeclares the goal goal", "the name proof_of_goal is declared twice"].

## Test: an empty submission and an unclosed comment are refused

Expect that submissionProblems("", "proof_of_goal", "goal") is equal to ["the submission is empty"].
Expect that submissionProblems("/- open", "proof_of_goal", "goal") is equal to ["an unterminated `/-` comment opens block 1"].

## Test: a statement defines its goal once, as a Prop, and asserts nothing

Expect that statementProblems(fixtureStatement(), "goal") is equal to [].
Expect that statementProblems("Axiom cheat : False.\n\nDefinition goal : Prop := False.", "goal") is equal to ["block 1 uses `Axiom`, which a statement may not use"].
Expect that statementProblems("Definition goal : Nat := Zero.", "goal") is equal to ["the goal goal must be defined as a `Prop`"].
Expect that statementProblems("Definition other : Prop := True.", "goal") is equal to ["the statement must define `goal` exactly once, as `Definition goal : Prop := …`"].

## Test: a name probe is the preamble and one Check

Expect that nameProbeDocument(fixtureStatement(), "goal") is equal to fixtureStatement() + "\n\nCheck goal.\n".
Expect that nameProbeDocument("", "False") is equal to "Check False.\n".

## Test: the failed block of a document can be shown back

Let doc be assembleProofDocument(fixtureStatement(), "Theorem proof_of_goal : goal := by intro n; rfl.", "proof_of_goal").
Expect that blockText(doc, 4) is equal to "Theorem proof_of_goal : goal := by intro n; rfl.".
Expect that blockText(doc, 99) is equal to "".

## Test: the submission is the one logos-kernel block, with its summary and suggestions

Let answer be """I will induct on n.

```logos-kernel
Theorem proof_of_goal : goal := by intro n; rfl.
```

Summary: closes the goal by computation.
Next: if rfl fails, induct on n.
Next: unfold Nat_add first.""".
Let x be extractSubmission(answer).
Expect that x's problem is equal to "".
Expect that x's block is equal to "Theorem proof_of_goal : goal := by intro n; rfl.".
Expect that x's summary is equal to "closes the goal by computation.".
Expect that x's suggestions is equal to ["if rfl fails, induct on n.", "unfold Nat_add first."].

## Test: other fences are ignored, even when they hold vernacular

Let answer be "For instance:\n```lean\ntheorem t : True := trivial\n```\n```logos-kernel\nTheorem proof_of_goal : goal := by exact I.\n```".
Let x be extractSubmission(answer).
Expect that x's problem is equal to "".
Expect that x's block is equal to "Theorem proof_of_goal : goal := by exact I.".

## Test: zero, two or an unclosed block is a refusal

Expect that (extractSubmission("no code here"))'s problem is equal to "the answer has no ```logos-kernel block".
Expect that (extractSubmission("```logos-kernel\nA.\n```\n```logos-kernel\nB.\n```"))'s problem is equal to "the answer has 2 ```logos-kernel blocks; exactly one is allowed".
Expect that (extractSubmission("```logos-kernel\nTheorem p : goal := by rfl."))'s problem is equal to "the ```logos-kernel block is never closed".
Expect that (extractSubmission("```logos-kernel\nA.\n```\n```logos-kernel\nB.\n```"))'s block is equal to "".
